Yes, your obligations change significantly. 

If you put your company name or trademark on a vendor's high-risk AI system, you will be legally considered the **provider** of that high-risk AI system (unless contractual arrangements stipulate otherwise) [ai_act, Article 25]. 

Consequently, you become subject to all the direct obligations of a provider under **Article 16**, which include [ai_act, Article 16, Article 25]:
* Ensuring the system complies with all high-risk requirements [ai_act, Article 16];
* Having a quality management system in place [ai_act, Article 16];
* Keeping required documentation and automatically generated logs [ai_act, Article 16];
* Ensuring the system undergoes the relevant conformity assessment procedure prior to putting it into service [ai_act, Article 16];
* Drawing up an EU declaration of conformity and affixing the CE marking [ai_act, Article 16];
* Meeting registration obligations in the EU database [ai_act, Article 16].
